                    "title": "Are the Humanities Ready for Data Sharing? - Ithaka S+R",
                    "content": "This issue brief will ultimately suggest that one key perspective that humanists can bring to larger debates about data sharing and open access research outputs is their uniquely well-developed infrastructure for the public sharing of knowledge creation, exemplified in the many public humanities initiatives that are a highly visible and vibrant part of humanities scholarship.[[9]](https://sr.ithaka.org/publications/are-the-humanities-ready-for-data-sharing/#post-318526-footnote-9) The formats, methods, and goals of public humanities initiatives vary widely, but many involve collaboration between academics, students, and diverse community partners.[[10]](https://sr.ithaka.org/publications/are-the-humanities-ready-for-data-sharing/#post-318526-footnote-10) Historically, the public humanities have been distinguished by their focus on placing “place communities, or other public audiences, at their core.”[[11]](https://sr.ithaka.org/publications/are-the-humanities-ready-for-data-sharing/#post-318526-footnote-11) Many recent public humanities projects emphasize community-driven, collaborative data generation efforts, in which knowledge is co-created _with_ community participants not _for_ the community.[[12]](https://sr.ithaka.org/publications/are-the-humanities-ready-for-data-sharing/#post-318526-footnote-12) These models of community generated data and co-created knowledge production can help inform and shape dynamic, equitable, and process-oriented data sharing across the academic sector. Some digital and public humanists are also articulating ethical and methodological frameworks to embrace community co-creation of data, in which researchers collaborate with community groups as users, stakeholders, and generators of data.[[25]](https://sr.ithaka.org/publications/are-the-humanities-ready-for-data-sharing/#post-318526-footnote-25) Often driven by social justice imperatives, many of these projects focus on the intersection of data and structural racism.",
